mso-border-left-alt:none;border-right:1.0000pt solid windowtext;mso-border-right-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;
border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;border-bottom:1.0000pt solid windowtext;
mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;">
                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:12.0000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="仿宋"><p><span style="font-family: 'times new roman', times, serif;">Pharmaceutical analysis is a methodological discipline dedicated to the study and development of comprehensive quality control methods for pharmaceuticals. It primarily employs chemical, physicochemical, or biochemical methods and techniques to investigate quality control approaches for synthetic or natural drugs with well-defined chemical structures, as well as their formulations.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-family: 'times new roman', times, serif;">The course begins with a general introduction to the core content of pharmaceutical analysis, covering essential topics such as identification, testing, and assay (quantitative determination) in the context of establishing pharmaceutical standards, and bioanalytical evaluation of drugs in biological samples. Following this foundational overview, the course proceeds to detailed discussions organized by drug structural classes.</span></p></font></span><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
